    "['tunnel-selector [parameter1] permit node [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-policy s1 permit node 10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'route-policy s1 permit node 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For the HUAWEI command `tunnel-selector s1 permit node 10`, the equivalent Cisco IOS XR configuration would be a **route policy** that matches and permits routes. The closest equivalent would be:\\n\\n##\\nroute-policy s1 permit node 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco doesn\\'t have a direct \"tunnel-selector\" concept, but route-policy is used for similar filtering/selection purposes.\\n2. The `apply tunnel-policy p1` part would need separate translation in Cisco (typically handled via route-policy actions or tunnel attributes).\\n3. The exact implementation may vary based on the specific Cisco platform (IOS/IOS-XR/NX-OS) and protocol context.', 'trans_template': 'route-policy s1 permit node 10'}]]": 1,
    "['apply tunnel-policy [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set tunnel-policy p1', 'parent_command': ['route-policy s1 permit node 10'], 'root': 'route-policy s1 permit node 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset tunnel-policy p1\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn\\'t have an exact 1:1 equivalent for Huawei\\'s tunnel policy application mechanism. The closest approximation would be using a route-map to influence tunnel selection, but this would require additional context about the tunnel policy\\'s purpose. The above translation represents a conceptual equivalent where a tunnel policy named \"p1\" is being applied. In Cisco implementations, this would typically be handled through more complex route-map configurations or tunnel selection mechanisms in MPLS TE.', 'trans_template': 'set tunnel-policy p1'}]]": 1,
