    "['tunnel-policy [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map p1 permit 10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'route-map p1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei `tunnel-policy p1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map p1 permit 10\\n match ip address 1\\n set ip next-hop load-share\\n##\\n  \\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco implementation uses route-map instead of Huawei's tunnel-policy\\n2. The load balancing behavior (load-balance-number 1 in Huawei) is approximated with set ip next-hop load-share in Cisco\\n3. You would need additional configuration (like an access-list 1) to complete the match criteria, as Cisco requires explicit matching\\n4. The BGP/LDP preference order would need to be handled through separate Cisco route-map sequences or policy routing configurations\", 'trans_template': 'route-map p1 permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match ip address 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'match ip address 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei `tunnel-policy p1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map p1 permit 10\\n match ip address 1\\n set ip next-hop load-share\\n##\\n  \\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco implementation uses route-map instead of Huawei's tunnel-policy\\n2. The load balancing behavior (load-balance-number 1 in Huawei) is approximated with set ip next-hop load-share in Cisco\\n3. You would need additional configuration (like an access-list 1) to complete the match criteria, as Cisco requires explicit matching\\n4. The BGP/LDP preference order would need to be handled through separate Cisco route-map sequences or policy routing configurations\", 'trans_template': 'match ip address 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ip next-hop load-share', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set ip next-hop load-share', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei `tunnel-policy p1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map p1 permit 10\\n match ip address 1\\n set ip next-hop load-share\\n##\\n  \\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco implementation uses route-map instead of Huawei's tunnel-policy\\n2. The load balancing behavior (load-balance-number 1 in Huawei) is approximated with set ip next-hop load-share in Cisco\\n3. You would need additional configuration (like an access-list 1) to complete the match criteria, as Cisco requires explicit matching\\n4. The BGP/LDP preference order would need to be handled through separate Cisco route-map sequences or policy routing configurations\", 'trans_template': 'set ip next-hop load-share'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel select-seq [parameter1] [parameter2] load-balance-number [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel preference bgp ldp load-balance 1', 'parent_command': ['route-map p1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map p1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ntunnel preference bgp ldp load-balance 1\\n##  \\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei `tunnel select-seq bgp ldp load-balance-number 1` command:\\n1. Specifies the tunnel selection sequence (BGP tunnels first, then LDP)\\n2. Enables load balancing with 1 path (effectively no load balancing, just sequential selection)\\n\\nThe Cisco equivalent is `tunnel preference bgp ldp load-balance 1` which:\\n1. Uses \"preference\" instead of \"select-seq\" to define the selection order\\n2. Uses \"load-balance\" instead of \"load-balance-number\"\\n3. Maintains the same parameters (bgp first, ldp second) and single-path selection', 'trans_template': 'tunnel preference bgp ldp load-balance 1'}]]": 1,
